                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Makes a tensor of random samples from the LAPLACE distribution: a pointed peak falling away evenly on both sides.\n\nIt is two exponential distributions back to back - one to the right of loc, its mirror to the left - so the peak comes to a point rather than the rounded top of a bell, and the tails, though falling fast, are heavier than a normal's. It models the DIFFERENCE between two random waiting times, and errors that are usually tiny but now and then moderate.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.dist.laplace <dim 0: int> <dim 1: int> ...\nWhole numbers give the shape of each batch of samples; with none\nthe node sends one sample at a time.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.dist.laplace 64 64\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin (button):\nThe small button at the top left of the node. Click it, or send it anything, for a fresh tensor of samples. Only this inlet makes the node send.\n\nshape (text, also an inlet):\nThe size of the tensor of samples, as whole numbers: [64, 64] is a 64 by 64 grid, [1000] a row of a thousand. Numbers typed after the node name set it (t.dist.laplace 64 64); with none it is [1], a single sample. A list or tensor of numbers, or a single number, sent into the inlet works too. A new shape does not send by itself - the next click uses it.\n\nloc (drag float, also an inlet):\nThe middle - the peak, the mean and the median. Any number. Default 0.\n\nscale (drag float, also an inlet):\nThe spread. Must be above 0. The average distance from loc is scale;\nthe standard deviation is about 1.41 times scale. Default 1.\n\nEach parameter is a drag float on the node and also an inlet. Setting one rebuilds the distribution but does NOT send; the next click samples from it. In the demo each slider goes through 't bang float', which passes the number to the parameter first and THEN clicks the node, so dragging a slider re-samples at once. A parameter that must be above 0 has a drag float that stops at 0.000001. A value the distribution cannot take (see each parameter) - 0 or below sent into such an inlet, say - prints a message in the console, and the node goes on sampling with the last parameters that worked.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrandom tensor:\nA float32 tensor on the cpu, in the shape set, filled with fresh independent samples on every click.\n\nTHE HISTOGRAM:\nt.histc divides the range from its min to its max into equal bins and counts how many of the 4096 samples land in each; the bar plot draws the counts, the first bin at the left. Its shape is the shape of the distribution. Samples outside min..max are not counted at all.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nload_bang draws the first 4096 samples when the page opens; click the node's own button for more. The heat_map (-3 dark, 3 bright) is mid-tones with a few strong cells. The histogram is a tent: a sharp point at loc and sides that slope down almost in straight lines (they are exponential curves). Compare it with t.dist.normal's rounded bell at the same settings. Drag scale to widen the tent, loc to move it.\n\nRELATED:\nt.dist.normal is the rounded bell; t.dist.exponential is one side of this tent; t.dist.cauchy has much heavier tails."
        },
